  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1226 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nDespite some of the increased troughiness out west, the jet stream \nis not in an especially favorable latitude to more convincingly shove \nridging out of our area. This will allow the ridge to reestablish \ntowards midweek, keeping the overall pattern of unseasonably warm \ntemperatures going for late September. Forecast highs remain in the \nmid to upper 90s with lows in the mid to upper 70s, aside from the \nupper 60s filtering into the higher terrain in the Hill Country. \nPeak afternoon heat indices are forecast to continue poking above \n100F for many. While the bulk thermal energy in the low to mid \ntroposphere isn't strongly suggestive of record-breaking heat, it \ndoes look like we will continue to straddle the upper ends of the \ndistribution for both daytime and nighttime temperatures at least \nthrough midweek.\n\nRain chances stay low in the forecast for next week, but aren't \nentirely out of the picture and there is the possibility that the \npattern could allow for recurring isolated rain chances during the \nearly half of the week. Depending on how the convection late \nSunday/early Monday evolves, residual boundaries and a band of \nhigher 700mb theta-e may stay draped over Central Texas before it \ngets cycled out by the ridge. For this reason, low rain chances up \nto around 25 percent remain in the forecast mainly for the \nafternoon/early evening hours during the upcoming workweek across \nvarying portions of South Central Texas. Recent runs of the EPS and \nAIGEFS lean towards slightly wetter depictions while the GEFS and \nECMWF AIFS have tended to be dry, so uncertainty is still high and \nwill hinge on how the aforementioned setup establishes. Regardless, \nwith synoptic forcing remaining weak, rains are not expected to be \nwidespread and most are likely to remain dry.",
